1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:10,978
In eighteen forty five, Britain sent two of the toughest ships in its navy into the Arctic, with a hundred and twenty nine men and enough food for three years.

2
00:00:11,478 --> 00:00:21,150
Their mission was to find the Northwest Passage, the sea route over the top of North America that explorers had been chasing for three hundred years.

3
00:00:21,650 --> 00:00:23,159
Not one of them came home.

4
00:00:23,659 --> 00:00:34,150
It took more than a hundred and sixty years, satellite maps, Inuit oral history, divers, and DNA, to even begin to understand what happened.

5
00:00:34,950 --> 00:00:36,834
Grab a warm drink for this one.

6
00:00:37,054 --> 00:00:38,298
It's a cold story.

7
00:00:39,698 --> 00:00:42,957
Hi, I'm Archie, and this is The Quiet Archive.

8
00:00:43,177 --> 00:00:46,884
Today's file is a big one, so I've cleared my whole desk.

9
00:00:47,104 --> 00:00:47,611
Well.

10
00:00:47,831 --> 00:00:48,691
Most of it.

11
00:00:49,191 --> 00:00:51,050
The lost Franklin expedition.

12
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:58,032
The Victorian era's greatest disaster at sea, and one of the strangest detective stories in history.

13
00:00:58,532 --> 00:01:01,458
We'll follow the evidence in the order it was found.

14
00:01:01,678 --> 00:01:03,396
A note in a pile of stones.

15
00:01:03,616 --> 00:01:05,722
Three graves on a frozen island.

16
00:01:05,942 --> 00:01:07,464
A lifeboat on a beach.

17
00:01:07,684 --> 00:01:11,485
And two shipwrecks, sitting under the ice for a century and a half.

18
00:01:11,985 --> 00:01:13,298
Let's open the file.

19
00:01:14,698 --> 00:01:16,497
First, why were they there at all?

20
00:01:16,717 --> 00:01:24,957
For centuries, European merchants dreamed of a shortcut from the Atlantic to the Pacific, over the top of North America.

21
00:01:25,457 --> 00:01:29,037
Sailing around Africa or South America took months.

22
00:01:29,257 --> 00:01:31,372
A northern route could save a fortune.

23
00:01:31,592 --> 00:01:37,744
The problem was that the route ran through a maze of islands, frozen solid for most of the year.

24
00:01:38,244 --> 00:01:41,344
Explorers had been trying since the fifteen hundreds.

25
00:01:41,564 --> 00:01:44,588
The Arctic had been winning since the fifteen hundreds.

26
00:01:45,088 --> 00:01:48,890
By the eighteen forties, Britain had mapped most of the maze.

27
00:01:49,110 --> 00:01:51,223
Only a few hundred miles were missing.

28
00:01:51,443 --> 00:01:56,649
The Admiralty decided one well-equipped expedition could finish the job.

29
00:01:58,049 --> 00:01:59,680
And they picked a famous name.

30
00:01:59,900 --> 00:02:01,182
Sir John Franklin.

31
00:02:01,682 --> 00:02:08,846
Twenty years earlier, Franklin had led an overland expedition in northern Canada that went horribly wrong.

32
00:02:09,066 --> 00:02:11,088
More than half of his party died.

33
00:02:11,308 --> 00:02:16,924
The survivors were so desperate for food they boiled and ate the leather from their own boots.

34
00:02:17,424 --> 00:02:20,123
Back in England, this made him a celebrity.

35
00:02:20,343 --> 00:02:22,724
People called him the man who ate his boots.

36
00:02:23,224 --> 00:02:25,313
It's a very Victorian kind of fame.

37
00:02:25,533 --> 00:02:31,610
Nearly starving to death in the Arctic was apparently the perfect qualification for going back.

38
00:02:32,110 --> 00:02:37,750
By eighteen forty five he was older, heavier, and hadn't been to the Arctic in years.

39
00:02:37,970 --> 00:02:43,008
But he was respected, well liked by his men, and he badly wanted the job.

40
00:02:44,408 --> 00:02:46,026
May eighteen forty five.

41
00:02:46,246 --> 00:02:50,835
The ships Erebus and Terror leave England, cheered off like rock stars.

42
00:02:51,335 --> 00:02:53,446
In command is Sir John Franklin.

43
00:02:53,666 --> 00:03:01,578
Fifty nine years old, a veteran of earlier Arctic journeys, famous at home, and very much the face of the mission.

44
00:03:02,078 --> 00:03:03,977
The ships were the best Britain had.

45
00:03:04,197 --> 00:03:07,899
Both had already survived an expedition to the Antarctic.

46
00:03:08,119 --> 00:03:14,384
They had reinforced hulls, heating pipes, and steam engines taken from railway locomotives.

47
00:03:14,884 --> 00:03:15,522
Yes.

48
00:03:15,742 --> 00:03:17,173
Actual train engines.

49
00:03:17,393 --> 00:03:19,429
Bolted into wooden sailing ships.

50
00:03:19,649 --> 00:03:22,601
The Victorians were never short on confidence.

51
00:03:23,101 --> 00:03:28,252
They carried three years of supplies, including about eight thousand tins of food.

52
00:03:28,752 --> 00:03:30,146
Remember those tins.

53
00:03:30,366 --> 00:03:32,584
They're going to come back into this story.

54
00:03:33,984 --> 00:03:42,160
In late July eighteen forty five, two whaling ships met Erebus and Terror in Baffin Bay, waiting for the ice to open.

55
00:03:42,660 --> 00:03:46,706
The whalers chatted with the crews, waved goodbye, and sailed off.

56
00:03:47,506 --> 00:03:50,849
That was the last time any European saw them alive.

57
00:03:52,249 --> 00:03:57,220
The first winter, they anchored at a small, bleak island called Beechey Island.

58
00:03:57,720 --> 00:03:59,775
Three young men died there that winter.

59
00:03:59,995 --> 00:04:03,231
John Torrington, John Hartnell, and William Braine.

60
00:04:03,451 --> 00:04:08,910
Their shipmates buried them in the frozen ground, with carefully painted wooden headboards.

61
00:04:09,410 --> 00:04:15,655
When search parties found those graves years later, they were the first hard evidence of the expedition.

62
00:04:15,875 --> 00:04:19,288
And the graves raised a question nobody could answer.

63
00:04:19,508 --> 00:04:21,008
Where did everyone else go?

64
00:04:21,508 --> 00:04:24,619
Because the crew left no message at Beechey Island.

65
00:04:24,839 --> 00:04:27,087
No note saying where they were heading next.

66
00:04:27,307 --> 00:04:30,462
For an expedition this organised, that was strange.

67
00:04:31,862 --> 00:04:34,878
Search crews found something else on Beechey Island.

68
00:04:35,098 --> 00:04:39,855
A neat pile of around seven hundred empty food tins, carefully stacked.

69
00:04:40,355 --> 00:04:44,495
Some had been filled with gravel and used as weights or markers.

70
00:04:44,715 --> 00:04:49,556
It suggests a crew that was organised, comfortable, and planning to come back.

71
00:04:49,776 --> 00:04:52,610
Nobody in that camp thought they were in trouble yet.

72
00:04:54,010 --> 00:04:55,398
We now know the answer.

73
00:04:55,618 --> 00:05:01,522
In eighteen forty six, the ships sailed south, down a channel toward King William Island.

74
00:05:02,022 --> 00:05:07,931
And in September of that year, off the island's northwest coast, the ice closed around them.

75
00:05:08,431 --> 00:05:10,083
Erebus and Terror were stuck.

76
00:05:10,303 --> 00:05:13,510
And as far as we know, they never sailed freely again.

77
00:05:14,910 --> 00:05:15,767
Picture this.

78
00:05:15,987 --> 00:05:24,367
Two wooden ships, frozen into a sheet of ice, with months of darkness, wind chills far below zero, and nowhere to go.

79
00:05:24,867 --> 00:05:27,888
The crews would have waited for the summer melt to free them.

80
00:05:28,108 --> 00:05:29,070
It didn't come.

81
00:05:29,290 --> 00:05:31,617
That summer, the ice simply didn't let go.

82
00:05:32,117 --> 00:05:37,974
And on the eleventh of June, eighteen forty seven, Sir John Franklin died aboard his ship.

83
00:05:38,194 --> 00:05:39,493
We don't know from what.

84
00:05:40,893 --> 00:05:44,129
Back in England, two years go by with no news.

85
00:05:44,349 --> 00:05:45,224
Then three.

86
00:05:45,724 --> 00:05:51,011
Franklin's wife, Lady Jane Franklin, was not a woman who waited quietly.

87
00:05:51,231 --> 00:05:59,605
She pushed the navy to search, wrote letters, raised money, and when the government slowed down, she paid for expeditions herself.

88
00:06:00,105 --> 00:06:03,550
The Admiralty offered a twenty thousand pound reward.

89
00:06:03,770 --> 00:06:06,697
Dozens of ships went looking over the next decade.

90
00:06:06,916 --> 00:06:13,347
Some of the search ships got stuck in the ice too, which is the most Arctic thing that could possibly happen.

91
00:06:13,847 --> 00:06:18,739
At one point, more people were looking for the Franklin expedition than had been on it.

92
00:06:20,139 --> 00:06:23,107
Some of the search methods were wonderfully strange.

93
00:06:23,607 --> 00:06:32,469
Search ships released hydrogen balloons that dropped printed messages over the ice, telling any survivors where to find supply depots.

94
00:06:32,969 --> 00:06:39,391
They also trapped Arctic foxes, fitted them with collars carrying messages, and let them go.

95
00:06:39,611 --> 00:06:47,151
The idea was that a fox might wander past starving sailors, who would catch it, read the collar, and find help.

96
00:06:47,651 --> 00:06:51,071
As far as we know, no fox ever delivered its mail.

97
00:06:51,291 --> 00:06:53,424
But you have to admire the optimism.

98
00:06:53,924 --> 00:06:58,862
Over the next decade, search ships mapped thousands of miles of new coastline.

99
00:06:59,082 --> 00:07:05,564
In a cruel twist, searching for Franklin filled in more of the Arctic map than Franklin ever did.

100
00:07:06,964 --> 00:07:12,000
The first real answer came in eighteen fifty four, from a Scottish explorer named John Rae.

101
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:16,411
Rae worked differently from most British officers.

102
00:07:16,631 --> 00:07:21,104
He travelled light, learned from the Inuit, and listened to what they told him.

103
00:07:21,604 --> 00:07:29,998
Inuit he met described seeing a group of white men dragging a boat south across the ice, years earlier, thin and starving.

104
00:07:30,218 --> 00:07:31,829
Later, they found bodies.

105
00:07:32,329 --> 00:07:35,533
And Rae reported something that shocked Victorian Britain.

106
00:07:35,753 --> 00:07:41,072
The evidence suggested that some of the last survivors had been driven to cannibalism.

107
00:07:42,472 --> 00:07:43,991
The reaction was furious.

108
00:07:44,211 --> 00:07:45,981
Lady Franklin rejected it.

109
00:07:46,201 --> 00:07:52,153
And the famous novelist Charles Dickens wrote that the Inuit accounts simply couldn't be trusted.

110
00:07:52,653 --> 00:07:53,687
Here's the thing.

111
00:07:53,907 --> 00:07:55,190
The Inuit were right.

112
00:07:55,690 --> 00:08:04,044
Modern forensic studies of bones from King William Island found cut marks consistent with exactly what Rae was told.

113
00:08:04,264 --> 00:08:08,301
The people who actually lived there had been telling the truth all along.

114
00:08:08,521 --> 00:08:10,297
Britain just didn't want to hear it.

115
00:08:11,697 --> 00:08:20,205
In eighteen fifty nine, an expedition paid for by Lady Franklin finally found the most important document in the whole story.

116
00:08:20,705 --> 00:08:29,456
At a place called Victory Point, on King William Island, a search party pulled apart a pile of stones and found a metal canister.

117
00:08:29,676 --> 00:08:31,890
Inside was a printed navy form.

118
00:08:32,390 --> 00:08:36,568
The first message on it, from May eighteen forty seven, was cheerful.

119
00:08:36,788 --> 00:08:38,612
The ships had wintered in the ice.

120
00:08:38,832 --> 00:08:39,524
All well.

121
00:08:40,024 --> 00:08:43,847
Then, written around the edges a year later, a second message.

122
00:08:44,347 --> 00:08:48,914
The ships had been deserted on the twenty second of April, eighteen forty eight.

123
00:08:49,134 --> 00:08:52,162
Sir John Franklin had died the previous June.

124
00:08:52,382 --> 00:08:54,974
Nine officers and fifteen men were dead.

125
00:08:55,194 --> 00:09:03,571
A hundred and five survivors, led by Captain Francis Crozier, would start the next day for Back's Fish River, on the mainland.

126
00:09:04,071 --> 00:09:07,558
The mood of that note changes in the space of one page.

127
00:09:07,778 --> 00:09:10,480
All well, becomes, we are leaving, on foot.

128
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:22,436
So imagine a hundred and five exhausted, sick men, dragging heavy wooden boats on sledges across the ice, toward a river hundreds of miles away.

129
00:09:22,936 --> 00:09:26,578
The same search party found one of those boats on a beach.

130
00:09:26,798 --> 00:09:32,984
Inside were two skeletons, and a strange collection of things the men had hauled across the ice.

131
00:09:33,484 --> 00:09:34,757
Silk handkerchiefs.

132
00:09:34,977 --> 00:09:35,936
Scented soap.

133
00:09:36,156 --> 00:09:37,050
Silverware.

134
00:09:37,270 --> 00:09:37,884
Books.

135
00:09:38,104 --> 00:09:38,898
Slippers.

136
00:09:39,398 --> 00:09:40,910
It's easy to laugh at that.

137
00:09:41,130 --> 00:09:45,290
But it also tells you how desperate and disoriented these men were.

138
00:09:45,510 --> 00:09:47,777
They were carrying their old lives with them.

139
00:09:48,277 --> 00:09:49,822
None of them reached safety.

140
00:09:51,222 --> 00:09:53,793
The Inuit stories didn't stop with the march.

141
00:09:54,013 --> 00:09:57,071
Families on King William Island remembered more.

142
00:09:57,571 --> 00:10:05,688
Some described a ship far to the south, near a place called Utjulik, that had drifted or been sailed there after the men left.

143
00:10:05,908 --> 00:10:10,298
Hunters said they boarded it and found a dead man lying inside.

144
00:10:10,798 --> 00:10:14,837
For a hundred and fifty years, most historians didn't look there.

145
00:10:15,057 --> 00:10:16,523
It was in the wrong place.

146
00:10:16,743 --> 00:10:20,533
The British records said the ships were abandoned much further north.

147
00:10:21,033 --> 00:10:21,800
Spoiler.

148
00:10:22,020 --> 00:10:23,596
The Inuit were right again.

149
00:10:24,996 --> 00:10:29,562
So why did a well-supplied, modern expedition fail so completely?

150
00:10:29,782 --> 00:10:31,288
There are several suspects.

151
00:10:31,788 --> 00:10:35,069
Suspect one, the cold, the darkness, and the ice.

152
00:10:35,289 --> 00:10:39,293
Two summers in a row, the ice didn't melt enough to free the ships.

153
00:10:39,513 --> 00:10:42,238
That alone is a death sentence in slow motion.

154
00:10:42,738 --> 00:10:44,223
Suspect two, scurvy.

155
00:10:44,443 --> 00:10:52,788
Without enough fresh food, vitamin C runs out, and wounds stop healing, teeth loosen, and people get weak and confused.

156
00:10:53,288 --> 00:10:54,905
Suspect three, the tins.

157
00:10:55,125 --> 00:10:56,054
Remember them?

158
00:10:57,454 --> 00:11:06,496
In nineteen eighty four, a team led by anthropologist Owen Beattie carefully exhumed the three sailors buried on Beechey Island.

159
00:11:06,996 --> 00:11:11,467
Thanks to the permafrost, the bodies were astonishingly well preserved.

160
00:11:11,967 --> 00:11:13,930
Tests found high levels of lead.

161
00:11:14,150 --> 00:11:15,822
One source looked obvious.

162
00:11:16,042 --> 00:11:24,002
The tinned food had been made in a rush, on a contract signed just weeks before the ships sailed, and sealed with lead solder.

163
00:11:24,502 --> 00:11:27,803
For decades, lead poisoning was the headline theory.

164
00:11:28,023 --> 00:11:36,015
It can cause weakness, confusion, and bad decisions, exactly what you'd expect from those desperate choices on the ice.

165
00:11:36,515 --> 00:11:38,115
But later studies pushed back.

166
00:11:38,335 --> 00:11:48,630
Some found that lead levels were not that unusual for people of the time, and pointed instead to malnutrition, tuberculosis, and other illnesses.

167
00:11:49,130 --> 00:11:52,223
The honest answer is probably all of the above.

168
00:11:52,443 --> 00:11:53,626
Lead didn't help.

169
00:11:53,846 --> 00:11:55,115
Scurvy didn't help.

170
00:11:55,335 --> 00:11:57,750
Starvation and cold finished the job.

171
00:11:59,150 --> 00:12:03,374
For a hundred and sixty nine years, the ships themselves were missing.

172
00:12:03,874 --> 00:12:08,947
The breakthrough came from combining modern technology with very old knowledge.

173
00:12:09,167 --> 00:12:21,714
Inuit oral histories, collected and studied by researchers including the Inuk historian Louie Kamookak, described a ship far to the south of where the British thought they had been abandoned.

174
00:12:22,214 --> 00:12:28,847
In September twenty fourteen, a Canadian search team found Erebus, sitting upright on the seabed.

175
00:12:29,347 --> 00:12:36,028
Two years later, Terror was found in a bay that, by sheer coincidence, was already named Terror Bay.

176
00:12:36,528 --> 00:12:38,324
You couldn't write that in a novel.

177
00:12:38,544 --> 00:12:40,219
An editor would send it back.

178
00:12:40,719 --> 00:12:43,706
Divers found Terror in remarkable condition.

179
00:12:43,926 --> 00:12:45,672
Glass windows still intact.

180
00:12:45,892 --> 00:12:47,234
Dishes on shelves.

181
00:12:47,454 --> 00:12:48,586
Bunks in place.

182
00:12:48,806 --> 00:12:51,688
A ship that had been waiting quietly under the ice.

183
00:12:53,088 --> 00:12:56,486
The latest chapter is being written right now, in labs.

184
00:12:56,986 --> 00:13:06,164
By comparing DNA from remains on King William Island with living descendants, researchers have started putting names back on the bones.

185
00:13:06,664 --> 00:13:14,253
In twenty twenty four, they identified Captain James Fitzjames, Franklin's second in command on Erebus.

186
00:13:14,473 --> 00:13:22,613
His remains also showed cut marks, confirming once again what Inuit witnesses had described in the eighteen fifties.

187
00:13:23,113 --> 00:13:24,847
More names have followed since.

188
00:13:25,067 --> 00:13:29,541
Each one is a sailor whose family can finally know where he ended up.

189
00:13:30,941 --> 00:13:32,513
And plenty is still missing.

190
00:13:33,013 --> 00:13:35,393
Franklin's grave has never been found.

191
00:13:35,613 --> 00:13:39,089
Nor has Captain Crozier, who led the survivors south.

192
00:13:39,309 --> 00:13:48,952
Nobody knows exactly how far the last men got, or whether any of them lived for years with Inuit families, as some later stories claimed.

193
00:13:49,452 --> 00:13:58,641
The wrecks are now protected as a national historic site, managed by Parks Canada together with Inuit guardians from the nearby community.

194
00:13:59,141 --> 00:14:01,885
Divers have recovered hundreds of objects.

195
00:14:02,105 --> 00:14:03,545
The big hope is paper.

196
00:14:03,765 --> 00:14:06,539
Cold, dark water can preserve documents.

197
00:14:06,759 --> 00:14:15,286
If a logbook or a diary survives somewhere inside Erebus or Terror, the men could finally tell this story in their own words.

198
00:14:16,686 --> 00:14:18,495
So here's the honest version.

199
00:14:18,995 --> 00:14:20,026
Documented.

200
00:14:20,246 --> 00:14:24,048
A hundred and twenty nine men sailed in eighteen forty five.

201
00:14:24,268 --> 00:14:28,827
The ships were trapped off King William Island in September eighteen forty six.

202
00:14:29,047 --> 00:14:31,834
Franklin died in June eighteen forty seven.

203
00:14:32,054 --> 00:14:37,379
The ships were deserted in April eighteen forty eight, and the survivors marched south.

204
00:14:37,599 --> 00:14:38,875
Nobody survived.

205
00:14:39,095 --> 00:14:44,948
Inuit testimony, the Victory Point note, forensic science, and the wrecks all fit together.

206
00:14:45,448 --> 00:14:46,200
Legend.

207
00:14:46,420 --> 00:14:47,805
A cursed expedition.

208
00:14:48,025 --> 00:14:49,775
Ghost ships sailing the Arctic.

209
00:14:49,995 --> 00:14:54,012
And the old Victorian idea that the Inuit couldn't be believed.

210
00:14:54,512 --> 00:14:56,601
What really happened was slower and sadder.

211
00:14:56,821 --> 00:15:03,601
Bad luck with the ice, poor food, sickness, and men who kept going far longer than anyone should have to.

212
00:15:05,001 --> 00:15:09,960
The thing that stays with me about this file is how long the answer was sitting there.

213
00:15:10,180 --> 00:15:13,149
The Inuit told the story in eighteen fifty four.

214
00:15:13,369 --> 00:15:16,839
It took us a hundred and sixty years to listen properly.

215
00:15:17,339 --> 00:15:19,339
What do you think was the biggest killer?

216
00:15:19,559 --> 00:15:22,400
The ice, the tins, or the decision to walk?

217
00:15:22,900 --> 00:15:24,175
Tell me in the comments.

218
00:15:24,675 --> 00:15:28,826
If you like true stories that history never quite finished, subscribe.

219
00:15:29,046 --> 00:15:33,591
I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and I'll see you in the next file.
